content.body:
Before you write your first endpoint, let's break down exactly what it looks like and why each piece exists. Read this carefully — it maps directly to what you will type in the next step.

An endpoint entry looks like this:

  getPosts: builder.query<Post[], void>({
    query: () => '/posts'
  })

Here is what each piece means:

  getPosts
    — The key name you choose. RTK Query will auto-generate a React hook from it:
      getPosts → useGetPostsQuery. The name drives the hook name.

  builder.query
    — Tells RTK this endpoint FETCHES data (read). If you were sending data (write), you would use builder.mutation instead.

  <Post[], void>
    — TypeScript generics. Post[] means "the response will be an array of Post objects". void means "this endpoint takes no argument when called".

  { query: () => '/posts' }
    — The config object. The query property is a function that returns the URL path. RTK appends this to your baseUrl:
      baseUrl '/api.com' + path '/posts' → full URL 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts'

You do not need to memorize this. In the next two steps you will type it in two small pieces so it settles naturally.

Read through the breakdown above. There is nothing to type here — click Next when ready.


---

// Full annotated endpoint for reference:
//
// getPosts              ← key name → becomes useGetPostsQuery hook
//   builder.query<      ← 'I am fetching data' (.mutation = writing data)
//     Post[],           ← response type: array of Post objects
//     void              ← argument type: no argument needed
//   >({
//     query: () => '/posts'  ← path appended to baseUrl
//   })
//
// baseUrl: 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com'
// path:    '/posts'
// result:  'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts'

